.resources/TASK-260720-2sltje/TASK-260720-2sltje_inputs.md:1:Extend the ReluxLibSSH2 fork (Dependencies/ReluxLibSSH2, pinned libssh2 a34302491 + OpenSSL 3.5.7, already carrying the TASK-260720-3vwls7 client-rekey export) with two more PUBLIC observation seams pinned libssh2 keeps internal (see TASK-260715-1ozsb6_server-rekey-keepalive-blocker.md for exact symbols). (1) SERVER-KEX OBSERVATION: libssh2 already runs server-initiated rekey (src/packet.c on inbound KEXINIT). Add a public typed observation (start/success/failure + a monotonic generation counter) so the adapter can truthfully emit server-rekey state/events — do NOT synthesize/guess state. (2) REPLY-CORRELATED KEEPALIVE/GLOBAL-REQUEST: add a public nonblocking op that sends a generic global request (e.g. keepalive@openssh.com) with want-reply + bounded payload, correlates the reply, and exposes reply completion so the adapter can compute RTT and detect timeout/miss, preserving libssh2 packet ordering/encryption/MAC. Both are OBSERVATION over existing behavior — no crypto/algorithm change. Keep the delta MINIMAL + allowlisted (extend the existing PATCH_MANIFEST + delta/rebase doc), checksum-verify pinned source before patching, preserve BSD-3-Clause + OpenSSL notices, rebuild the static extension-safe XCFramework via the 1g9cyt seam. Add deterministic tests: a server-initiated rekey observed with correct transitions + generation increment, and a keepalive request->reply RTT plus a timeout/miss path (a local sshd fixture like the accepted rekey test is fine). Concrete libssh2/OpenSSL symbols stay inside the fork/adapter boundary (make validate-core enforces). This is the LAST known contract clause for libssh2; if implementing either seam requires more than a bounded observation patch, STOP-THE-LINE with the exact delta + a note that the M0-vs-M3 contract-scope question needs the human.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-14qi1v_host-trust-and-auth-experience/TASK-260715-3pxgxx_add-host-trust-and-auth-security-tests/README.md:7:In scope: first use, approved reuse, changed/revoked key, algorithm changes, explicit replacement, stale generations, provenance/timestamps, lane identity mismatch, provider-before-auth ordering, app suspension/termination, duplicate requests, profile conflict, passphrase lifecycle, Keychain errors, auth rejection, retry/cancel, serialization bounds, logs/diagnostics scans, and repeated cycles. Out of scope: visual UI assertions, production hosts/credentials, SSH engine conformance already owned by M0/M1, throughput, and release signing.
